This is a placeholder, and it deliberately does not live in res/raw: Android's
resource merger rejects any filename with more than one dot in it, so an
".example" sitting in there fails the build before it starts.

Copy your own Caddy root certificate to

    app/app/src/main/res/raw/caddy_ca.crt

before building. The real file is gitignored so nobody ends up shipping theirs,
which is why res/raw looks empty in a fresh clone.

On the server, Caddy keeps its root at:

    /var/lib/caddy/.local/share/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt

So, from the repo root:

    scp root@<your-server>:/var/lib/caddy/.local/share/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt \
        app/app/src/main/res/raw/caddy_ca.crt

The app trusts this certificate and nothing else -- not the public CA roots.
That is deliberate: it means the app talks to your server or it talks to
nobody. If Caddy ever regenerates its CA, re-copy the file and rebuild.
